const DASH_LESSON_INFO = {
  "8175-L01": {"n":1,"task":"The Seven Eras of Marketing (Timeline Analysis)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-39","text":"I can explain how marketing has changed over time by giving the time period and main focus of each of the seven eras, from Trade to Collaboration."},{"id":"8175-43","text":"I can describe the seven functions of marketing by naming all seven and showing two at work with real sports or entertainment examples."},{"id":"8175-41","text":"I can explain the marketing concept by comparing the Selling Era with the Marketing Company Era and saying what businesses started doing for customers instead of tricking them."},{"id":"8175-11","text":"I can think about the big picture by explaining in my prediction how AI, virtual reality, or privacy laws could change how businesses treat the people they market to."},{"id":"8175-45","text":"I can connect marketing's past to its future by linking my timeline and next-era prediction to how sports or entertainment marketing has changed and might change next."},{"id":"8175-47","text":"I can analyze trends by using at least one real trend, such as AI, virtual reality, or privacy laws, to back up my prediction and next-era name."}],"parts":["For each of the seven eras, from the Trade Era to the Collaboration Era, you give its time period and its primary focus in one sentence.","You explain the difference between the Selling Era and the Marketing Company Era, and say what businesses started doing for customers instead of tricking them into buying.","You name the next era of marketing and back up your prediction with at least one real trend: AI, virtual reality or privacy laws.","You name all seven functions of marketing, pick two, and give a real sports or entertainment example of each."]},
  "8175-L02": {"n":2,"task":"The Passion Test (Intrinsic Motivation)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-107","text":"I can identify a personal interest that drives me by naming one thing I do because I love it and explaining in 2 to 3 sentences why I enjoy it."},{"id":"8175-3","text":"I can explain what drives me by showing that I keep doing this thing on my own, without money, rewards, or anyone pushing me, and saying what keeps me going."}]},
  "8175-L03": {"n":3,"task":"Defining Your Personal Brand (The CEO of YOU)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-64","text":"I can position myself like a product by stating my USP, explaining why a team should pick me over 100 other applicants, and naming who would benefit most."},{"id":"8175-107","text":"I can identify my personal strengths by naming my strengths and attributes and explaining what value they let me give to people."},{"id":"8175-12","text":"I can plan my career path by connecting my brand statement to the kind of job or team I want and showing how my strengths could get me there."},{"id":"8175-56","text":"I can explain branding by pulling my value, USP, and audience together into a personal brand statement that sounds like me and shows what I stand for."}]},
  "8175-L04": {"n":4,"task":"Personal Brand Analysis (The Celebrity Case Study)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-56","text":"I can explain a personal brand by describing what makes my public figure well known and how others describe them, so a reader gets a clear picture."},{"id":"8175-105","text":"I can identify the parts of a SWOT analysis by giving one strength, one weakness, one opportunity, and one threat for my public figure, each in a complete sentence."},{"id":"8175-2","text":"I can think critically by deciding whether my public figure's reputation matches who they really seem to be and backing up my answer with reasons from what I found."},{"id":"8175-57","text":"I can explain branding in sports and entertainment by showing how my public figure's personal brand connects to their sport, show, or industry and grows their career."},{"id":"8175-86","text":"I can analyze the effects of negative media by explaining how the threat I named could spread through news or social media and hurt my public figure's brand."}]},
  "8175-L05": {"n":5,"task":"Sources of Support (Developing Grit)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-12","text":"I can manage my own support system by naming the communities that support me, what they have done to help me succeed, and whether any hold me back."},{"id":"8175-3","text":"I can take initiative by naming a specific step I can take on my own to join a new community that could support me."},{"id":"8175-109","text":"I can plan how to build my network by naming a new sports, entertainment, or events group I could join and saying how I would get involved and stay connected."}]},
  "8175-L06": {"n":6,"task":"Skill Development Plan (The \"Soft Skills\" Gap)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-20","text":"I can plan to grow professionally by writing a 100-word plan with specific ways to practice patience, face-to-face communication, and resilience before starting a new job."},{"id":"8175-112","text":"I can prepare for a job interview by naming one specific thing I could do to help myself in an interview and explaining how it would help."},{"id":"8175-21","text":"I can write clearly by keeping my plan to about 100 words, answering all three questions, and checking it for spelling and grammar."},{"id":"8175-5","text":"I can show a strong work ethic by explaining in my plan how I'll keep practicing these skills over time, even when it is hard or no one is checking."}]},
  "8175-L07": {"n":7,"task":"Ethical Dilemmas in the Workplace","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-4","text":"I can act with integrity by naming my scenario and outlining exactly what I would do to act honestly and fairly even when it is hard."},{"id":"8175-51","text":"I can identify unethical business practices by naming what is unethical in my scenario and explaining why it is wrong, not just against the rules."},{"id":"8175-20","text":"I can act professionally by writing my planned response in a calm, respectful tone I could really use at work, without insults or slang."},{"id":"8175-6","text":"I can resolve conflict by explaining how I would handle the people in my scenario so the problem gets solved without making the conflict worse."},{"id":"8175-49","text":"I can explain how communication shapes work relationships by saying what I would tell the people in my scenario and how saying it that way protects our relationship."}]},
  "8175-L08": {"n":8,"task":"The \"Rule of Six\" Slide Challenge","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-50","text":"I can present like a professional by building a 3 to 5 slide deck that follows the Rule of Six, with speaker notes that add to my slides."},{"id":"8175-48","text":"I can identify types of communication by labeling in my speaker notes one place I use verbal, one I use nonverbal, and one I use visual communication."},{"id":"8175-18","text":"I can use presentation software well by making high-contrast slides in PowerPoint or Google Slides with a light background, dark font, no more than 2 images, and minimal animations."},{"id":"8175-7","text":"I can speak clearly to an audience by writing my speaker notes the way I would actually say them out loud, in clear words that fit my audience."}]},
  "8175-L09": {"n":9,"task":"What IS Branding? (The Mentor Challenge)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-56","text":"I can explain branding by saying what it is not, defining it simply, explaining why people buy a real brand like Apple, and showing how a brand is a promise."},{"id":"8175-54","text":"I can translate business language into everyday words by explaining branding so someone with no business background understands it, including any business term I use."},{"id":"8175-21","text":"I can write a clear explanation of at least 250 words with an introduction, an example, the promise, and a conclusion, checked for spelling and grammar."},{"id":"8175-2","text":"I can think critically about brands by explaining the real reasons people choose my example brand over others instead of only describing it."},{"id":"8175-57","text":"I can explain branding in sports, entertainment, or events by using an example from one of them to show how a brand builds trust with fans."}]},
  "8175-L10": {"n":10,"task":"Service Experience Reflection (The Gold Standard)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-52","text":"I can explain why customer service matters by describing what a company did to stand out, how it made me feel, and how it changed the value of my purchase."},{"id":"8175-55","text":"I can describe how to handle a customer complaint by using the LAST steps to explain how the employee should have handled a time service went wrong."},{"id":"8175-21","text":"I can write a clear reflection of at least 10 complete sentences that answers all four questions and is checked for spelling and grammar."},{"id":"8175-9","text":"I can recognize good customer service by naming the helpful, courteous, or knowledgeable actions that made the good service work and were missing when service went wrong."},{"id":"8175-11","text":"I can think about the big picture by explaining how great or bad service from one employee affects the whole company and whether customers come back."},{"id":"8175-46","text":"I can explain hospitality by showing how the way I was welcomed and treated as a guest shaped my whole experience, not just the product."},{"id":"8175-53","text":"I can compare types of customer interaction by saying what kind of service I got in each story, such as self-service or full service, and how it affected my experience."}]},
  "8175-L11": {"n":11,"task":"CHHS Brand Audit (The Home Field Advantage)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-57","text":"I can evaluate a school brand by explaining what the Cavalier logo, colors, and reputation say to the community and naming specific areas for improvement."},{"id":"8175-42","text":"I can explain target marketing by identifying who Clover Hill High School serves using both demographics and psychographics in at least 75 words."},{"id":"8175-59","text":"I can explain reasons for rebranding by showing how current cultural and societal trends, such as social media or mental health awareness, could push the school to rebrand."},{"id":"8175-21","text":"I can write a clear essay of at least 250 words that meets the word count for each section and is checked for spelling and grammar."},{"id":"8175-47","text":"I can analyze trends by naming at least one current trend and explaining how it changes what students and the community expect from the school's brand."}]},
  "8175-L12": {"n":12,"task":"SWOT Analysis (The War Room)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-105","text":"I can analyze a team or company's position by naming 2 internal strengths, 2 weaknesses, 2 outside opportunities and 2 threats it faces."},{"id":"8175-101","text":"I can explain competition by naming at least one real competitor or competing option in my analysis and explaining how it affects my team or company."},{"id":"8175-102","text":"I can plan a marketing move by giving the General Manager one specific recommendation and showing which parts of my SWOT analysis it comes from."}]},
  "8175-L13": {"n":13,"task":"CHHS Rebrand (The Pitch and Focus Group Prep)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-103","text":"I can plan useful market research by writing 3 specific focus group questions that show whether students really connect with my brand ideas without leading them to an answer."},{"id":"8175-50","text":"I can present to start a conversation by building a 5 to 7 slide deck that covers every required slide and uses more images and visuals than text."},{"id":"8175-1","text":"I can think creatively by creating a new tagline or theme for the school and showing a desired future that is clearly different from the current reality."},{"id":"8175-85","text":"I can explain internal and external public relations by showing on my rollout slide how I'd announce the new brand inside the school and to families and the community."}]},
  "8175-L14": {"n":14,"task":"Local Business Deep Dive (The Consultant's Report)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-69","text":"I can compare types of business ownership by naming my business's ownership type and explaining how it changes who makes decisions and who takes the risk."},{"id":"8175-47","text":"I can analyze trends by predicting at least one change for my business in the next 5 years and explaining which AI tool or trend would cause it."},{"id":"8175-50","text":"I can present like a consultant by building a minimal-text slide deck from my script and presenting it to the class."},{"id":"8175-7","text":"I can speak clearly to an audience by writing my word-for-word script so it sounds natural spoken aloud and is clear to my classmates."},{"id":"8175-10","text":"I can collaborate with a partner by using at least one piece of feedback from my Day 3 partner to improve my final presentation."},{"id":"8175-15","text":"I can use reliable information by basing my brand analysis on real facts about the business and saying where those facts came from."},{"id":"8175-23","text":"I can examine how a business plans by explaining in one or two sentences how my business plans ahead."},{"id":"8175-24","text":"I can examine how a business is managed by explaining in one or two sentences how my business is run."},{"id":"8175-25","text":"I can examine financial responsibility by explaining in one or two sentences how my business handles its money."},{"id":"8175-26","text":"I can identify the skills workers need by explaining in one or two sentences what my business's workers must be able to do in their jobs."},{"id":"8175-27","text":"I can explain the technology behind a business by describing in one or two sentences the technology my business runs on."},{"id":"8175-28","text":"I can examine labor issues by naming one labor issue my business faces and explaining it in one or two sentences."},{"id":"8175-30","text":"I can examine health, safety, and environmental issues by naming one my business faces and explaining it in one or two sentences."},{"id":"8175-44","text":"I can explain why the sports and entertainment industry matters by showing why my business matters to the people who go there and to the local community."},{"id":"8175-68","text":"I can explain how a sports or entertainment business works by showing how my business's brand, products, offerings, planning, management, and money fit together to keep it running."},{"id":"8175-106","text":"I can identify jobs in the industry by naming specific jobs at my business when I describe the skills its workers need."}]},
  "8175-L15": {"n":15,"task":"Olymponomics (The Economics of Hosting)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-98","text":"I can explain the economics of hosting the Olympics by arguing it is a smart investment or a trap, using the cost, the revenue, and what happened to the stadiums."},{"id":"8175-15","text":"I can use sources well by listing at least 3 sources at the bottom of my essay and using specific facts from them as examples."},{"id":"8175-21","text":"I can write persuasively in a 200-word essay that states my position clearly, supports it with examples, and is checked for spelling and grammar."},{"id":"8175-29","text":"I can examine community issues by explaining how hosting affected the host city and its people, such as the empty stadiums in Athens or Rio."},{"id":"8175-99","text":"I can explain how the business cycle affects big events by saying whether the host country's economy was growing or in a recession and how that affected the Games' results."},{"id":"8175-100","text":"I can explain supply and demand by comparing demand for tickets, hotels, or stadiums during the Games with afterward and how that affected the city's money."}],"parts":["For each of the five topics (cost, revenue, white elephants, 1984 Los Angeles, the economy), you write down a specific fact and the source it came from.","You take a side, smart investment or financial trap, and back it with the cost, the revenue and what happened to the stadiums."]},
  "8175-L16": {"n":16,"task":"The Pizza Break-Even Challenge","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-19","text":"I can calculate a break-even point by showing my work to find how many pizzas I must sell to cover the 500 dollar rent at 10 and at 7 dollars."},{"id":"8175-67","text":"I can choose a pricing strategy by picking price skimming or market penetration for my new pizza brand and justifying my choice in 2 sentences."},{"id":"8175-95","text":"I can explain bundling by creating a combo, giving it a price, and explaining why a fan would buy the bundle instead of the pizza alone."},{"id":"8175-40","text":"I can identify the Ps of the marketing mix by describing my stand's product, place, and promotion in one sentence each."},{"id":"8175-66","text":"I can explain a pricing decision by using my break-even numbers or what fans will pay to show why my chosen price makes sense."},{"id":"8175-97","text":"I can explain stadium concessions by showing how my stand's combo or setup fits what fans want to buy at the football stadium."},{"id":"8175-101","text":"I can explain competition by showing how my price or combo would help my stand win fans over other food choices at the stadium."}],"parts":["You show your work and find how many pizzas you must sell to cover the 500 dollar rent at 10 dollars (Scenario A) and at 7 dollars (Scenario B).","You pick price skimming or market penetration for a new pizza brand and justify your choice in 2 sentences."]},
  "8175-L17": {"n":17,"task":"The Ticketmaster Problem (Distribution Channels)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-70","text":"I can explain a distribution channel by identifying the producer, intermediary, and consumer in a concert ticket sale and each one's role in getting the ticket to the fan."},{"id":"8175-51","text":"I can judge whether a business practice is ethical by taking a clear stand in my 100-word opinion piece on bot scalping and saying whether it should be illegal."},{"id":"8175-71","text":"I can identify distribution challenges by explaining why a stadium versus a club changes how tickets get to fans."},{"id":"8175-100","text":"I can explain supply and demand by using it to show why the scalper can resell tickets for double the price."}]},
  "8175-L18": {"n":18,"task":"The First E-Sports Olympic Games","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-60","text":"I can plan a new product by giving my sport a name and concept, explaining how it works and its rules, and why it would make a good Olympic event."},{"id":"8175-42","text":"I can explain target marketing by describing who watches my sport using demographics and using psychographics to explain why my event appeals to them."},{"id":"8175-87","text":"I can explain sponsorship by naming specific companies that would sponsor my sport and explaining why each would want to be connected to it."},{"id":"8175-10","text":"I can collaborate with a partner by each of us building clear parts of the proposal and combining them into one project that fits together."},{"id":"8175-65","text":"I can plan for things outside my control by naming two things that could hurt my event and explaining how I would prepare for each."},{"id":"8175-76","text":"I can explain advertising at events by showing how advertising will appear at my event and how it helps my sponsors reach fans."}]},
  "8175-L19": {"n":19,"task":"The Album Drop (Product and Promotion)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-93","text":"I can describe the parts of a promotional campaign by mapping a two-month hype timeline and pitching a customer profile, branding message, ad mockup, PR talking points and success metrics."},{"id":"8175-73","text":"I can explain how different kinds of promotion work together by mixing them into my weekly schedule, including a public appearance, a fan interaction and an interview."},{"id":"8175-104","text":"I can explain why a marketing plan's return on investment matters by estimating my two-month plan's cost and showing how sales, streams or followers per dollar prove it paid off."},{"id":"8175-22","text":"I can work safely by following the class rules for cutting tools, hot glue and paint while altering my sleeve and vinyl, and keeping my station clear."},{"id":"8175-42","text":"I can explain target marketing by building my customer profile around one specific group of fans and aiming my branding message and ad mockup at them."},{"id":"8175-60","text":"I can plan a product fans will value by altering my sleeve and vinyl so every change adds perceived value and the cover clearly shows the artist and album name."},{"id":"8175-61","text":"I can explain a product's life cycle by planning my timeline so promotion builds interest the month before release and keeps the album selling the month after."},{"id":"8175-75","text":"I can identify the parts of an advertisement by making an ad mockup that clearly shows the album, the artist and a reason for fans to listen or buy."},{"id":"8175-77","text":"I can explain what an ad is trying to do and how to measure it by tying my success metrics to my ad mockup."},{"id":"8175-84","text":"I can use public relations tools by writing PR talking points the band could use in the interview and public appearance to get positive attention for the album."}]},
  "8175-L20": {"n":20,"task":"My Cause My Cleats (The Power of Strategic Partnership)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-83","text":"I can explain public relations in sports by showing, as a marketer would, how the My Cause My Cleats partnership helps both the athlete's personal brand and the non profit."},{"id":"8175-11","text":"I can see the bigger picture by connecting why my athlete chose this cause to what both the athlete and the non profit gain from it."},{"id":"8175-90","text":"I can identify sponsorship trends by explaining how my athlete and non profit teaming up for My Cause My Cleats is co-branding with a cause."},{"id":"8175-2","text":"I can think critically by backing up my answer about why the athlete chose this cause with facts I found, not just a guess."},{"id":"8175-8","text":"I can show respect for others by describing my athlete's cause and the people it serves with care."}]},
  "8175-L21": {"n":21,"task":"The Naming Rights Bid and Ambush Marketing","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-87","text":"I can explain how sponsorships work by writing a 5-sentence naming rights proposal for why my chosen real company wants its name on the Clover Hill football stadium."},{"id":"8175-88","text":"I can compare sponsorships and endorsements by explaining the difference between them and giving one reason a brand might choose each."},{"id":"8175-58","text":"I can explain brand protection by defining ambush marketing and judging in 2 sentences whether it is genius or dishonest toward the brands that paid to sponsor the event."},{"id":"8175-89","text":"I can identify the rules that protect sponsors by explaining what fee or rule ambush marketers get around when they advertise near the Olympics without paying the Olympic Committee."},{"id":"8175-91","text":"I can explain how stakeholders are connected by showing what the brand, the school and its fans would each gain from the naming rights deal."}],"parts":["You choose a real company and write a 5-sentence proposal explaining why it wants its name on the Clover Hill football stadium.","You define ambush marketing and judge in 2 sentences whether it is genius or dishonest toward the brands that paid to sponsor the event."]},
  "8175-L22": {"n":22,"task":"My Cause My Cleats (The Campaign Pitch)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-93","text":"I can plan a promotional campaign by explaining how my cleats will get attention, proposing one fundraising idea and picking a sales promotion that fits my athlete and non profit."},{"id":"8175-92","text":"I can weigh the pros and cons of sales promotions by choosing one giveaway, contest, coupon or limited-edition item and giving one advantage and one disadvantage of it."},{"id":"8175-1","text":"I can create original ideas by designing my own cleats and one merch item that shows the athlete, the non profit and the NFL team at once."},{"id":"8175-50","text":"I can present a business idea clearly by pitching my concept to the NFL in about 5 to 7 slides, each covering one part of the plan."},{"id":"8175-74","text":"I can identify ways to get a campaign seen by naming the specific places or channels my campaign will use to get the cleats noticed."},{"id":"8175-87","text":"I can explain what a sponsorship or endorsement deal gives each side by showing what the athlete and the non profit each gain from their partnership."}]},
  "8175-L23": {"n":23,"task":"The Industry Publication (Create Your Own Magazine)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-1","text":"I can create an original publication by designing an eye-catching front cover and laying out our articles and ads so the magazine looks finished."},{"id":"8175-75","text":"I can design an effective advertisement by making a colorful, eye-catching ad that clearly shows what is being sold and is aimed at our demographic."},{"id":"8175-82","text":"I can explain how digital media makes money by describing two ways our magazine could earn online, such as digital ads, subscriptions, sponsored posts or video."},{"id":"8175-10","text":"I can work well on a team by doing my assigned role, writing my own article and giving useful feedback in the Day 2 Peer Review."},{"id":"8175-14","text":"I can manage my time by meeting each daily checkpoint, from our Topic and Target Market on Day 1 to the finished, bound project on Day 5."},{"id":"8175-50","text":"I can present to an audience by helping give a group presentation that clearly highlights our demographic, articles and pricing strategy."},{"id":"8175-81","text":"I can compare traditional and digital marketing by explaining how making money online for our magazine differs from selling the printed, bound copy."}]},
  "8175-L24": {"n":24,"task":"Sales Training Camp (Methods and Roleplay)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-94","text":"I can explain the types of selling by defining personal and non-personal selling and listing 2 benefits and 2 negatives of each."},{"id":"8175-96","text":"I can explain the selling process by listing its 6 steps in the right order and finishing the sentence \"Selling is ...\"."},{"id":"8175-7","text":"I can listen and respond in a sales conversation by recording what I sold, my opening line, the Customer's two rejections, my replies, and the line that won the \"Yes.\""},{"id":"8175-10","text":"I can work with a partner by playing Salesperson and Customer, following the two-rejection rule, and reflecting on whether I had to change my strategy."}],"parts":["You define personal selling and non-personal selling and list 2 benefits and 2 negatives of each.","You list the 6 steps of the selling process in the right order and finish the sentence \"Selling is ...\".","You write down what you sold, your opening line, the Customer's two rejections, what you said after each one, and the line that got the \"Yes.\""]},
  "8175-L25": {"n":25,"task":"Black History Month Entrepreneur Valentine","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-15","text":"I can research a person using reliable information by putting three specific, accurate facts about one Black entrepreneur's background or business ventures into my card."},{"id":"8175-1","text":"I can be creative by designing an original Valentine's Day card, with my own verse and layout, that works the three facts in."},{"id":"8175-8","text":"I can show respect for others' backgrounds by honoring my entrepreneur's background and impact on their industry with care in my card."}]},
  "8175-L26": {"n":26,"task":"Preparing Your Elevator Pitch (The 30 Second Commercial)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-12","text":"I can describe what I bring to a career by filling in two to three specific examples from my own life for Education, Top Strengths, Achievements and Academic Projects."},{"id":"8175-109","text":"I can start a networking plan by naming two to three types of companies that interest me and two to three people I look up to."},{"id":"8175-7","text":"I can prepare to speak about myself by choosing Interesting Facts that set me apart and would sound strong said out loud in a 30-second pitch."}]},
  "8175-L27": {"n":27,"task":"Spring Break Field Report (Marketing in the Wild)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-72","text":"I can explain promotion by giving specific marketing examples I noticed over Spring Break and describing how one campaign likely went from an idea to what I saw."},{"id":"8175-79","text":"I can identify ways brands keep customers by explaining how one brand tried to keep me after the first ad, like a follow, email sign-up, loyalty app or discount code."},{"id":"8175-104","text":"I can explain why a campaign's return on investment matters by describing how the company could tell whether the campaign earned back what it cost."},{"id":"8175-2","text":"I can think critically by reasoning through who likely approved the budget and who designed the graphics, rather than only describing the ad."},{"id":"8175-78","text":"I can describe online marketing tools by naming the ones I ran into, such as sponsored posts, email sign-ups or loyalty apps, and saying how each worked."},{"id":"8175-80","text":"I can explain how brands pick platforms by saying why the brand chose the app, site or feed where I saw its ad to reach people like me."}]},
  "8175-L28": {"n":28,"task":"Designing Unbiased Surveys (Google Forms)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-62","text":"I can explain what market research is for by writing two quantitative questions and one qualitative question that would find out what customers want from the new service."},{"id":"8175-2","text":"I can spot and fix bias by checking each question and explaining in one or two sentences which question I rewrote and what I changed to make it neutral."},{"id":"8175-17","text":"I can use Google Forms by building my three questions, with answer choices for the two quantitative ones, and pasting the form's send link."},{"id":"8175-103","text":"I can explain why research matters to a marketing plan by asking questions whose answers could actually help us decide how to design the new service."}]},
  "8175-L29": {"n":29,"task":"Survey Quality Control (The Audit)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-105","text":"I can use a SWOT analysis by critiquing the class survey under Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities and Threats, naming specific questions or problems in each part."},{"id":"8175-10","text":"I can help improve a team's shared work by giving specific changes in The Fix that would make the class survey clearer, more consistent or less biased."},{"id":"8175-103","text":"I can explain why research matters to a marketing plan by saying in Opportunities what else the survey could learn about our customers and how knowing it would help."}]},
  "8175-L30": {"n":30,"task":"Data-Driven Segmentation (Survey Build)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-63","text":"I can create a survey for primary research by writing 10 closed and 2 open questions and naming which ones check each market segment exists and how to reach it."},{"id":"8175-17","text":"I can use Google Forms by building all 12 questions with the right question types and pasting the form's send link with my questions typed below."},{"id":"8175-103","text":"I can explain why research matters to a marketing plan by writing questions about reaching each segment that would help decide where and how to market to it."}]},
  "8175-L31": {"n":31,"task":"Survey Analysis (Data Driven Decisions)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-2","text":"I can solve a problem using data by giving one concrete suggestion for how our league or event should be run differently, based on what the survey feedback shows."},{"id":"8175-103","text":"I can use research to back a marketing decision by citing specific numbers or responses from the survey to justify my suggestion."},{"id":"8175-16","text":"I can protect private information by leaving out every respondent's name and email and explaining in one sentence why survey data must be kept private."},{"id":"8175-19","text":"I can use math to read survey results by giving counts or percentages from the summary charts when I describe my three interesting findings."}]},
  "8175-L32": {"n":32,"task":"Future Proofing Your Career (AI and the Job Market)","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-13","text":"I can plan to adapt to a changing job market by explaining in my 200-word essay how AI may reshape work, how I can prepare, and one job to research."},{"id":"8175-15","text":"I can find and use reliable information by listing the five fastest growing industries predicted for the next decade and citing at least three different sources from my research."}]},
  "8175-L33": {"n":33,"task":"The 6-Second Resume","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-110","text":"I can write a professional resume by making a one-page resume with the full header, education, experience bullets that each start with an action verb, and soft and hard skills."},{"id":"8175-108","text":"I can find job search resources by locating one real job or internship posting on TeamWork Online, Indeed, Handshake or a team's careers page and pasting its link."},{"id":"8175-34","text":"I can stay safe online by using a professional email address and sharing only the contact details my resume header asks for."},{"id":"8175-111","text":"I can complete a job application by filling in my school, graduation date, GPA and experience accurately, the same way I would on a real application."}]},
  "8175-L34": {"n":34,"task":"Interview Skills Practice Plan","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-112","text":"I can describe how a job interview works by explaining what surprised me in the interview skills video and naming one specific thing I could do in an interview."},{"id":"8175-3","text":"I can take charge of my own growth by writing a 100-word plan for practicing my body language, tone and confidence on my own before an interview."}]},
  "8175-L35": {"n":35,"task":"The Google Interview Challenge","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-112","text":"I can answer interview questions well by giving the question and a polished final answer for all five Interview Warmup questions."},{"id":"8175-13","text":"I can learn from feedback by writing, under each final answer, one piece of feedback the tool gave me and what I changed because of it."},{"id":"8175-7","text":"I can speak and listen in an interview by answering all five questions out loud in the tool and using its feedback before writing my final answers."},{"id":"8175-113","text":"I can practice a mock interview by treating the Interview Warmup like a real one, answering each question out loud as I would for an employer."}]},
  "8175-L36": {"n":36,"task":"The Course Brochure (Honest Feedback)","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8175-1","text":"I can design creatively by making a brochure with a creative logo on the front cover and a panel that showcases a memorable project from the course."},{"id":"8175-106","text":"I can identify careers in sports, entertainment and events by naming three careers this class might prepare me for on Panel Three."},{"id":"8175-20","text":"I can give feedback professionally by sharing honest pros and cons of the teaching approach on Panel Four, worded respectfully as in a workplace review."}]},
  "8177-the-business-of-the-game": {"n":1,"task":"The Corporate Scouting Report","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-86","text":"I can identify how a team or company makes money by naming its top three revenue sources and explaining in a sentence how each one brings in money."},{"id":"8177-52","text":"I can explain how a team or company is organized by naming who owns it, its type of ownership, and how I know."},{"id":"8177-50","text":"I can explain how a business affects its local economy by describing one specific way my team or company affects its home city, good or bad."},{"id":"8177-15","text":"I can find and cite reliable information by giving my team or company’s current value with its source and listing every source with the website name and link."},{"id":"8177-24","text":"I can explain how ownership shapes management by showing who makes my team or company’s big decisions and who takes the risk."},{"id":"8177-53","text":"I can compare for-profit and nonprofit ownership by explaining one advantage or disadvantage of how my team or company is owned versus the Green Bay Packers."},{"id":"8177-94","text":"I can explain what competes for fans’ attendance by naming my team or company’s biggest indirect competitor for a Friday night’s money and why fans might choose it."}],"parts":["You name the team or company and give its current value with the source it came from (website name and link).","You name who owns it, say whether it is a sole proprietorship, partnership or corporation, and explain how you know.","You name its top three sources of revenue and explain in a sentence how each one brings in money.","You describe one specific way the business affects its home city, good or bad, such as jobs, charity, a stadium paid for with tax money, or game-day traffic.","You name its biggest indirect competitor for a Friday night's money and explain why fans might choose it instead."]},
  "8177-the-4-ps-of-the-fan-experience": {"n":2,"task":"The Signature Item","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-97","text":"I can plan a marketing mix by writing my signature item’s product, price, place and promotion, and explaining what happens to the other three Ps if I change one."},{"id":"8177-46","text":"I can explain how an on-site item earns money for a venue by giving my signature item’s price, cost to make and profit each, and why fans will pay it."},{"id":"8177-10","text":"I can collaborate with my marketing team by writing the ideas we agreed on in my own words and naming the P I’ll present in our pitch."},{"id":"8177-47","text":"I can explain how online and app sales affect a venue by describing how fans would buy my signature item in the team app or online and why that fits."}],"parts":["You describe your signature item and what makes it special to this team or venue.","You give the price, the cost to make it and the profit on each one (price minus cost), and explain why fans will pay that price.","You say exactly where fans can buy the item and why you chose that spot.","You describe one specific way you will launch the item and get fans talking about it.","You pick one P and explain what would happen to each of the other three if you changed it.","You name the P you will present in your team's pitch."]},
  "8177-chhs-school-store-company-description": {"n":3,"task":"The Cavalier Shop Company Description","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-21","text":"I can write a professional company description for the Cavalier Shop in at least five complete sentences, without slang or ‘I think’ statements."},{"id":"8177-56","text":"I can identify the building blocks of a new business by saying what the Cavalier Shop sells, how it’s run, its target market, and its USP in a bolded sentence."},{"id":"8177-23","text":"I can plan before I write by answering every Plan It question so my company description is built around the Cavalier Shop’s USP."},{"id":"8177-11","text":"I can connect the Cavalier Shop to the bigger school community by explaining how it supports school spirit and events and what customers really support when they buy."},{"id":"8177-93","text":"I can explain what motivates customers by giving two reasons a student or parent would buy Cavalier gear at the shop for a spirit day or game night, not online."}],"parts":["You answer every Plan It question: what the store sells, who its customers are, two reasons to buy here, and its USP.","You write a company description of at least 5 sentences in a professional voice, with no slang and no \"I think.\"","Your USP sentence is bolded or underlined, and it names one thing the Cavalier Shop offers that big stores can't."]},
  "8177-what-is-branding-reflection-essay": {"n":4,"task":"What Is Branding?","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-21","text":"I can write a clear essay of at least 500 words that defines branding simply, gives three examples in their own paragraphs, and ends with why branding matters."},{"id":"8177-2","text":"I can think critically about my learning and writing by naming what three class projects taught me about branding and rewriting every sentence a beginner wouldn’t understand."},{"id":"8177-11","text":"I can see the big picture of branding by explaining why it matters both to a business and to me as someone who buys from brands."},{"id":"8177-97","text":"I can explain how marketing choices build a brand by using my 4 Ps signature item to show how its price, place and promotion shaped its brand."}],"parts":["You list three projects or examples from this class and write one sentence for each about what it taught you about branding.","Your essay is at least 500 words, opens with a simple definition of branding, gives three examples in their own paragraphs, and explains each marketing word in plain language.","You underline each sentence a beginner wouldn't understand and rewrite it in plain language."]},
  "8177-school-store-planogram-customer-experience": {"n":5,"task":"The Cavalier Shop Planogram","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-45","text":"I can plan how a store’s space works by mapping traffic-flow arrows, preventing a lunch-rush bottleneck, and labeling three merchandise zones, the hot zone and impulse buys."},{"id":"8177-22","text":"I can design a safe store by marking every exit and the 36-inch clear walkways on my map, with nothing blocking an exit or door."},{"id":"8177-95","text":"I can analyze staffing needs by marking where each staff member stands during a shift and explaining how each spot helps customers or prevents loss."},{"id":"8177-1","text":"I can improve on an existing design by explaining how my layout beats the Cavalier Shop’s current setup, like a new path or hot zone display."},{"id":"8177-11","text":"I can connect a store’s layout to its brand by explaining how my atmospherics, like lighting, music, colors or signs, make the store feel like part of the Cavalier brand."},{"id":"8177-72","text":"I can explain how the ADA shapes store design by showing how my 36-inch walkways let customers who use wheelchairs move through the store."},{"id":"8177-73","text":"I can explain how legal safety rules shape operations by showing how 36-inch walkways and never blocking an exit decided where I put shelves, racks and tables."},{"id":"8177-92","text":"I can plan a store like a small venue by explaining how my layout handles a busy lunch rush from the moment customers enter until they check out."}],"parts":["You add a top-down map of the store that shows the entrance, the exits, the register and every shelf, rack and table, each one labeled.","You draw arrows for the path most customers take and explain where a bottleneck could form during a lunch rush and how your layout prevents it.","You label at least three merchandise zones, say where your hot zone is, what goes there and why, and say where impulse buys go and why.","You mark every exit and the 36-inch clear walkways on your map, with nothing blocking an exit or a door."]},
  "8177-product-pitch-financial-analysis": {"n":6,"task":"The Buyer's Product Pitch","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-19","text":"I can calculate whether a product will make money by correctly showing cost per finished item, profit per item, margin, total batch cost, break-even point, expected profit and turnover."},{"id":"8177-96","text":"I can figure out production costs by naming a real supplier with a link, listing each material’s cost, minimum order and shipping, and estimating items per class period."},{"id":"8177-93","text":"I can explain what makes customers buy by describing who will want my product, whether CHHS students, families or fans, and when, like a season, big game or spirit week."},{"id":"8177-2","text":"I can justify a price by explaining the strategy behind my retail price and how it covers my cost, leaves a profit and stays a price students will actually pay."},{"id":"8177-7","text":"I can communicate a pitch clearly by writing it so the General Manager quickly understands what the product is, who will buy it and why it will make money."}],"parts":["You name the product and say what it is, what it looks like and what makes it different from what the Cavalier Shop already sells.","You say who will want your product (CHHS students, families or fans) and when they will want it, like a season, a big game or spirit week, and why.","You name a real supplier with a link, list each material's cost, the minimum order and shipping, and estimate how many the class can make in one class period.","You give a retail price and explain the strategy behind it: how it covers your cost, leaves a profit and is still a price students will pay.","You show each calculation correctly: cost per item, profit per item, margin, total batch cost, break-even point, expected profit if the batch sells, and how long it takes to sell out.","Your deck pitches the product in 3 to 5 slides: the product, who buys it and when, the cost and price, and the bottom line. Every slide follows the Rule of Six, has good contrast and is easy to read, and the speaker notes say what you'd tell the General Manager."]},
  "8177-the-economics-of-the-arena": {"n":7,"task":"The Pricing Strategy Brief","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-19","text":"I can calculate ticket revenue by showing price × tickets sold at all five prices for each of the three games."},{"id":"8177-94","text":"I can explain how demand affects ticket prices by using supply, demand and scarcity to show why the playoff game can charge much more than Snowy Tuesday."},{"id":"8177-2","text":"I can make and defend a decision by choosing a price for each game using my math and taking a side on whether the $100 and $20 seats are fair."},{"id":"8177-86","text":"I can identify revenue beyond tickets by explaining what the team gains, like concession sales, when it picks a lower price to fill the arena."}],"parts":["You show price × tickets sold, worked correctly, at all five prices for each of the three games.","You choose a price for each game and explain each choice using the revenue it brings in.","You use supply, demand and scarcity to explain why the playoff game can charge much more than Snowy Tuesday with the same team and arena.","You take a clear side on whether the $100 Saturday seat and the $20 Tuesday seat are fair and give at least one reason that backs it up."]},
  "8177-market-research-survey-design": {"n":8,"task":"The Cavalier Shop Survey","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-83","text":"I can build a market research survey by making the Google Form, pasting its link, and explaining whether each question collects quantitative or qualitative data and what the store learns."},{"id":"8177-2","text":"I can spot and fix bias by explaining what’s wrong with each of the three biased questions and rewriting each one so it’s neutral."},{"id":"8177-79","text":"I can identify sources of market research data by choosing one more primary and one secondary source for the Cavalier Shop and saying what each adds that the survey can’t."},{"id":"8177-18","text":"I can use Google Forms like a researcher by setting up checkboxes for up to two, a 1-to-5 linear scale and a short answer, with email collection turned off."},{"id":"8177-81","text":"I can choose a target market by picking students, parents or staff and explaining why that group matters most to the Cavalier Shop."},{"id":"8177-93","text":"I can explain what motivates customers by saying what Question 2 will tell the store about whether supporting CHHS student activities makes people want to buy."}],"parts":["You pick one group (students, parents or staff) and say what makes them different from the other two as customers.","Your form has all three questions set up as shown: checkboxes for Question 1 (choose up to two), a 1–5 scale with its two labels for Question 2, a short answer for Question 3.","For each of the three questions, you say whether it collects quantitative or qualitative data and one thing the store would learn from the answers.","For each biased question, you name what pushes people (a leading word, a leading question or a loaded choice) and rewrite it so someone with the opposite opinion could answer just as easily.","You name one more primary source and one secondary source the Cavalier Shop could really use."]},
  "8177-survey-deployment-statistical-significance": {"n":9,"task":"Survey Fieldwork","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-83","text":"I can carry out a market research survey by explaining why the store needs 323 responses, not 5 or 10 friends, and logging the date, place and count each time."},{"id":"8177-14","text":"I can manage my time and resources by planning when and where I’ll collect my 15 responses and who I’ll ask, reaching beyond my closest friends."},{"id":"8177-4","text":"I can act with integrity by explaining why every response must be real and keeping a log with no names and each person counted only once."},{"id":"8177-7","text":"I can ask people for help clearly by writing a 10-second pitch that says who I am, what the survey is for and how quick it is."},{"id":"8177-82","text":"I can reflect on how a research project ran by describing what worked best in collecting my responses and what I would do differently next time."}],"parts":["You explain why the store needs 323 responses instead of 5 or 10 friends and name one thing that could go wrong if it decided from too few answers.","You write a 10-second pitch that says who you are, what the survey is for and how quick it is.","You say when and where you will collect responses (only at approved times) and who you will ask, reaching beyond your closest friends.","Your log gives the date, the place and how many people took the survey each time you collected responses.","You say what worked best in collecting your responses and one thing you would do differently next time."]},
  "8177-survey-results-analysis": {"n":10,"task":"The Data Analysis Report","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-84","text":"I can evaluate survey results by stating three findings from the real data, each with its number, and explaining what each one means for the Cavalier Shop."},{"id":"8177-2","text":"I can solve a business problem with data by recommending one specific change to the Cavalier Shop’s layout, hours, products or promotion and backing it with numbers from my findings."},{"id":"8177-78","text":"I can explain why marketing functions need research by writing one sentence for each of three functions on how the survey helps it or what a future survey should ask."},{"id":"8177-21","text":"I can write a professional report of at least 250 words, in complete sentences with no slang, that I could hand to the principal."},{"id":"8177-45","text":"I can explain how a change affects store operations by showing how my recommended layout or hours change would change how the Cavalier Shop runs day to day."},{"id":"8177-77","text":"I can explain the value of market research by showing how the survey results help the Cavalier Shop make better marketing decisions than guessing."}],"parts":["You give three findings from the real survey results, each with its number, and explain what each one means for the Cavalier Shop.","You recommend one specific change to the Cavalier Shop's layout, hours, products or promotion, specific enough that the store could do it.","You explain how at least one of your data points supports your recommendation, using its numbers.","For three marketing functions, you write one sentence each on how the survey results help it or one question a future survey should ask for it."]},
  "8177-promotional-flyer-design": {"n":11,"task":"The Cavalier Shop Flyer","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-1","text":"I can design an eye-catching black-and-white flyer with a headline that stops people walking, the logo, hours and location, and three items with their real prices."},{"id":"8177-18","text":"I can use design software by building my flyer in Canva, Adobe Express or Google Slides, checking the logo in grayscale, and adding the PDF or its link."},{"id":"8177-21","text":"I can write about design clearly by typing my flyer’s exact words and explaining what I made biggest and why, how I used contrast and where I left negative space."},{"id":"8177-14","text":"I can make the most of limited resources by designing my flyer to print well on the school’s black-and-white copier, avoiding light grays and thin text."},{"id":"8177-97","text":"I can plan promotion that brings customers in by explaining how my flyer’s headline and items will get students walking the hallway to come into the Cavalier Shop."}],"parts":["Your black-and-white flyer has a headline that would stop someone walking, the Cavalier Shop logo, the hours and location, and three items with their real prices.","In a short paragraph, you explain what you made biggest and why, how you used contrast, and where you left negative space on purpose.","You say what you could and couldn't read from far away and name at least one change you made to the flyer after the test."]},
  "8177-the-hype-machine": {"n":12,"task":"Pack the House","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-97","text":"I can plan a promotional campaign with a paid ad mockup with audience, placement and cost, a PR stunt the news would cover, and a sales promotion with a deadline."},{"id":"8177-42","text":"I can make a manager’s campaign decisions by budgeting the $5,000 item by item without going over and explaining why every promise would hold up on game night."}],"parts":["Your Instagram, TikTok or poster ad mockup says exactly who it's aimed at, where it will run and how much of the $5,000 it costs.","You pitch an event before the game that NBC12 or the school paper would cover for free and explain why it's news, not just an ad.","You create a short-term deal (a contest, giveaway or flash sale) that gets people to buy tickets early, with its deadline.","You list how you'd spend the $5,000 item by item, and the items add up to $5,000 or less.","You go through your campaign's promises and explain how each one would really hold up on game night, so no one is misled."]},
  "8177-the-richmond-festival-blueprint": {"n":13,"task":"The Richmond Festival Blueprint","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-92","text":"I can plan an event step by step by answering every vision, logistics and business question specifically, including a Richmond location and why I chose it."},{"id":"8177-95","text":"I can analyze staffing needs by listing five specific jobs to hire for and explaining what each one does at my event."},{"id":"8177-67","text":"I can manage risk by naming one thing that could go wrong at my event and explaining exactly how I’d prepare for it."},{"id":"8177-1","text":"I can create an event concept by inventing a name, theme and vibe that would make my crowd want to pay to come."},{"id":"8177-68","text":"I can plan for things I can’t control by explaining how a rainstorm, power outage or similar problem could change my plans and how my event would still run."},{"id":"8177-87","text":"I can identify event expenses by naming what will cost the most for my event, like performers, venue rental, insurance or security, and saying why."},{"id":"8177-96","text":"I can analyze how an event pays for itself by listing where my event’s money comes in and what costs the most."},{"id":"8177-97","text":"I can market an event to its crowd by writing a 3-sentence social media post, with a hashtag, announcing that tickets are on sale."}]},
  "8177-game-day-takeover": {"n":14,"task":"The Game Day Takeover","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-99","text":"I can recognize the people behind an event by listing everyone who helped and what each did, writing one staff member a specific thank-you note, and drafting a customer thank-you."},{"id":"8177-95","text":"I can plan staffing for an event by building a staffing chart showing every pop-up role, who fills it and who backs them up."},{"id":"8177-10","text":"I can work as part of a team by writing my role and shift in the run of show and describing how I helped the team during my shift."},{"id":"8177-58","text":"I can serve customers well by describing one customer I served during my shift and one problem that came up and how it was handled."},{"id":"8177-100","text":"I can manage an event by building a setup-to-cleanup run of show with the busy times marked, a supply list, and one risk with a backup plan."},{"id":"8177-101","text":"I can judge an event’s success by comparing its real numbers with our goals and naming one thing to keep and one to change, using the results."}],"parts":["You build the pop-up's timeline from setup to cleanup using the game schedule, with the busy times (before the game and halftime) marked.","Your staffing chart lists every role (shift lead, cashier, sales floor/runner, inventory and setup), who fills it and who backs them up.","You list everyone who helped and what each did, type a thank-you note to one staff member saying what they did and why it mattered, and draft a public thank-you.","You compare each goal from Part 2 with the real number from the event log and say whether it was met.","You name one thing to keep and one thing to change for the next event and explain each one using the event's results."]},
  "8177-tournament-sponsorship-proposal": {"n":15,"task":"The Sponsorship Letter","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-21","text":"I can write a formal business letter with hook, connection, value and call to action paragraphs in correct letter format, proofreading to fix every error."},{"id":"8177-15","text":"I can research a real local business by finding its sponsorship decision-maker’s name, title and mailing address and explaining why its customers match our tournament crowd."},{"id":"8177-97","text":"I can pitch a sponsor’s marketing value by saying exactly what the sponsor gets for its money in my Value paragraph, using the benefits my teacher gave me."},{"id":"8177-20","text":"I can write professionally by keeping my letter free of slang, greeting the contact by last name with a colon, and closing with ‘Sincerely,’ and my name."},{"id":"8177-96","text":"I can explain how sponsor money supports an event by saying in my letter what the sponsor’s money would help pay for at the tournament."}],"parts":["You name a real Richmond or Chesterfield business, its sponsorship decision-maker's name, title and mailing address, and why its customers are the people at our tournament.","You write a one-page formal business letter with a Hook, Connection, Value and Call to Action paragraph, in correct business letter format.","Your final letter has no slang, greets the contact by last name with a colon, and closes with \"Sincerely,\" and your name."]},
  "8177-the-millionaire-s-strike": {"n":16,"task":"The Millionaire's Strike","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-28","text":"I can analyze a labor dispute by giving my side's position and reason on all four issues, what each side gave up, and why players are hard to replace."},{"id":"8177-70","text":"I can explain how labor law shapes pro sports by naming the right the National Labor Relations Act gives players and why owners want the draft and salary cap bargained."},{"id":"8177-50","text":"I can explain how a lost season hurts a local economy by naming three Richmond businesses or hourly jobs and how each one's income depends on game days."},{"id":"8177-49","text":"I can explain how lost game-day paychecks could spread into less spending and more unemployment around the city."},{"id":"8177-69","text":"I can identify the terms of a contract by recording the new collective bargaining agreement's final terms on all four issues and explaining what it settles for both sides."},{"id":"8177-71","text":"I can explain what a players' union is and how bargaining together gives athletes power they wouldn't have negotiating one at a time."}],"parts":["You say which side you're on and, for each of the four issues, what your side wants and one reason it's fair.","You record the final agreement on all four issues (revenue sharing, season length, minimum salary, overseas play), plus your notes of each offer and counteroffer on one issue, in order, with who made it.","You name three specific Richmond businesses or hourly jobs and, for each one, explain exactly how its income depends on game days.","You explain why owners can't just replace striking players (their rare, specialized skills) and why being able to play overseas gives players extra bargaining power.","You name the right the National Labor Relations Act gives players and explain why owners want the draft and salary cap written into a CBA, where antitrust law can't touch them."]},
  "8177-the-stadium-economy": {"n":17,"task":"The City Council Recommendation","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-49","text":"I can analyze a stadium's economic effects by explaining its impact on consumer spending and GDP, choosing the new-spending estimate I trust, and showing how interest and inflation raise costs."},{"id":"8177-50","text":"I can judge whether a stadium pays off for the county by comparing its yearly loan payment with the yearly taxes on truly new spending."},{"id":"8177-45","text":"I can plan a facility's size by recommending a seating capacity and using the law of diminishing returns and the team's 4,500-fan average to explain why."},{"id":"8177-29","text":"I can examine a community issue by explaining who in Chesterfield County would pay for the stadium and who would benefit from it."},{"id":"8177-66","text":"I can describe a stadium's value to the community by naming a benefit beyond money and saying whether it changes my verdict."}],"parts":["You state Approve or Deny (or approve with conditions) and back it by comparing the county's yearly loan payment with the yearly taxes on truly new spending.","You explain how the stadium would affect consumer spending and the county's GDP.","You explain with the report's numbers how the interest rate (6% vs 3%) and inflation (about $18 million a year of delay) make the county's share more expensive.","You recommend a seating capacity and use the law of diminishing returns and the team's 4,500-fan average to explain why it beats 12,000 seats."]},
  "8177-the-cloud-behind-the-game": {"n":18,"task":"The Data Center Decision","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-65","text":"I can analyze media bias by explaining three ways Story A and Story B use the same facts to leave opposite impressions, then writing a balanced headline and three-sentence story."},{"id":"8177-64","text":"I can weigh economic, social and environmental concerns in a decision using a T-chart of three pros and three cons and fact-sheet facts behind my data center recommendation."},{"id":"8177-44","text":"I can organize a business's roles by drawing my startup's organizational chart with five or more launch roles, who reports to whom, and how it changes by year three."},{"id":"8177-15","text":"I can check information against a source by marking every number or claim in both stories “true,” “true but missing context” or “not on the fact sheet.”"},{"id":"8177-48","text":"I can explain how esports or another mostly online event depends on data centers in my step-by-step walkthrough or my startup idea."},{"id":"8177-55","text":"I can describe the entrepreneurial mindset by naming the problem or need my startup spots and explaining which parts of that mindset it takes to go after it."},{"id":"8177-57","text":"I can explain how a small business drives growth by saying how many jobs my startup would create and how it would help other local businesses grow."}],"parts":["You explain step by step how one streamed game, mobile ticket, esports tournament or fantasy app depends on data centers, and sort all eight uses into \"needs AI\" and \"doesn't need AI.\"","Your T-chart has at least three pros and three cons from the fact sheet, with at least one economic, one social and one environmental point, and you explain how the AI option changes it.","You explain at least three ways Story A and Story B leave opposite impressions from the same facts, then write a balanced headline and three-sentence story using only fact-sheet facts.","Your organizational chart shows at least five roles at launch and who reports to whom, and you say how it changes by year three.","You say whether the county should support, oppose or support with conditions (naming them), and whether to allow the AI option, in one paragraph backed by fact-sheet evidence."]},
  "8177-the-global-expansion": {"n":19,"task":"The International Expansion Proposal","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-74","text":"I can explain outside factors abroad by showing why my brand has a market in my city, converting a ticket price at today's exchange rate, and solving one overseas problem."},{"id":"8177-80","text":"I can adapt marketing to a culture by describing one change to my event or marketing that fits local culture and explaining why local fans would respond."},{"id":"8177-51","text":"I can explain how a cultural or political factor in my chosen country, like local customs or tax rules, could affect how much money the event makes."}],"parts":["You name your brand and a foreign city and explain why there's a market for it there: fans, the sport's popularity, population or something from your research.","You name the local currency, set a ticket price in it, convert it to dollars at today's real exchange rate with your math shown, and name the website where you found the rate.","You describe one specific change to your event or marketing that fits the local culture and explain why local fans would respond to it, with no stereotypes.","You describe one real problem of taking a team or event overseas (customs, jet lag and time zones, tax rules) and a workable way to handle it."]},
  "8177-brand-board": {"n":20,"task":"The Brand Board","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-1","text":"I can create an original brand by inventing an event or venue and building its brand board: five colors, one font, two logo versions, my three words and four images."},{"id":"8177-45","text":"I can connect a brand to a facility by explaining how my colors and logo would show up inside the venue: on walls, in lighting, or on signs and screens."},{"id":"8177-42","text":"I can make brand decisions others can follow by explaining how my colors, font and images match my three words, so anyone making a sign, shirt or post stays consistent."},{"id":"8177-11","text":"I can see the big picture by explaining how one consistent look across tickets, signs, jerseys and posts would shape what customers think of my whole event or venue."},{"id":"8177-21","text":"I can write clear, complete sentences that use brand, brand identity, color palette, typography, logo and atmospherics accurately when I explain my idea."},{"id":"8177-93","text":"I can explain what motivates customers by describing who my event or venue is for and what in the experience my four images show would make them want to come."},{"id":"8177-97","text":"I can plan how a brand promotes an event by showing how mine would appear in social posts, digital tickets or jerseys to get people to come."}],"parts":["You invent an original event or venue and describe it in two or three sentences, including who it is for.","You choose three adjectives for how you want customers to feel and explain why you chose each one.","Your board has all of it: five colors, one font, two logo versions, your three words and four images of the customer's experience.","For your colors, your font and your images, you explain how each choice matches your three words."]},
  "8177-employee-handbook-operations": {"n":21,"task":"The Cavalier Shop Operations Brief","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-25","text":"I can explain financial controls by describing the Z-Out in my own words and a real problem each of the Two-Person and scan-everything rules prevents."},{"id":"8177-95","text":"I can analyze staffing needs by giving one responsibility for each of the four roles and explaining what goes wrong in the shop if nobody does it."},{"id":"8177-58","text":"I can serve customers during a rush by writing exactly what I'd say to the waiting line, keeping it polite, calm and professional."},{"id":"8177-4","text":"I can explain how the Two-Person Rule and scanning everything, even a free giveaway, keep the shop honest with its cash and stock."},{"id":"8177-5","text":"I can explain why work ethic matters by showing what goes wrong for the whole team when one person skips their part, like a restock or leaving the drawer open."},{"id":"8177-10","text":"I can coordinate a team by saying who does what during the rush so we handle the waiting line and the jammed register together."},{"id":"8177-14","text":"I can prioritize under pressure by putting my rush plan in a clear order so the line keeps moving while the register gets fixed."},{"id":"8177-20","text":"I can speak professionally by using polished language, not slang, in what I say to the waiting customers, the way the handbook's greeting does."},{"id":"8177-45","text":"I can explain how a venue opens by listing the four opening procedures in the right order, starting with clearing the 36-inch walkways."},{"id":"8177-72","text":"I can explain how the ADA shapes a facility by saying why the 36-inch walkways have to be completely clear of boxes before the shop opens."},{"id":"8177-73","text":"I can connect laws to operations by naming ADA and fire code as the rules behind the first opening step when I list the opening procedures."}],"parts":["For each of the four roles (General Manager, Shift Manager, Sales Associate, Inventory Specialist), you give one responsibility and explain what goes wrong if nobody does it.","You explain the Z-Out in your own words: what gets counted, that the float is subtracted, and that the result is checked against the daily sales report.","You explain the Two-Person Rule and the scan-everything rule and, for each one, describe a real problem it prevents.","You write exactly what you'd say to the customers waiting in line, and it is polite, calm and professional."]},
  "8177-secure-the-perimeter": {"n":22,"task":"The Emergency Action Plan","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-90","text":"I can explain a venue's security needs by sending fans somewhere safe from lightning and mapping each section's route with arrows so the main gate avoids a bottleneck."},{"id":"8177-91","text":"I can plan for an emergency by writing an emergency action plan with a calm three-sentence PA announcement and explaining how one school drill is an emergency action plan."},{"id":"8177-89","text":"I can explain how different staff keep people safe by saying where the police officers, guest services staff and athletic trainers go and what each does during the evacuation."},{"id":"8177-27","text":"I can explain how a tool like two-way radios or the PA system helps my staff move fans safely during the evacuation."},{"id":"8177-30","text":"I can explain a safety hazard by saying why staying on metal bleachers during lightning is dangerous and why the place I send fans is safer."},{"id":"8177-68","text":"I can plan for what I can't control, like the gym holding only about 1,500 people when 3,000 fans need shelter."}],"parts":["You say where the fans should go and why that place is safe from lightning.","Your map shows each section's route with arrows (home, visitor) and how you keep the main gate from becoming a bottleneck, such as using the north emergency gate.","You say where the police officers, the guest services staff and the athletic trainers go and what each group does during the evacuation.","Your announcement is exactly three sentences, keeps people calm, and tells each section exactly where to go.","You name one school drill (fire, tornado or lockdown) and explain how it is an emergency action plan for the school as a venue."]},
  "8177-the-back-room": {"n":23,"task":"The Receiving Report","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-41","text":"I can check a shipment by comparing the purchase order, packing slip and my count for all four items and listing every missing, wrong, damaged and backordered problem."},{"id":"8177-14","text":"I can manage our resources by making a table of how many usable items of each kind we can print on and explaining why each uncounted item can't be used."},{"id":"8177-26","text":"I can write a professional email to Blank Canvas Wholesale that lists each problem, gives the purchase order number and credit amount, and asks for replacements and a ship date."},{"id":"8177-27","text":"I can explain how production depends on the right supplies by showing why the black t-shirts or the backordered transfer paper keep us from printing."}],"parts":["For all four items you compare the purchase order, packing slip and your count, and list every problem: missing hoodies, wrong-color t-shirts, torn towels, backordered paper.","Your table shows how many usable items of each kind we received and can print on, and you explain why each item you didn't count can't be used.","Your professional email to Blank Canvas Wholesale lists each problem, gives PO #CS-1042 and the credit amount, and asks for replacements and a ship date for the backorder."]},
  "8177-the-battle-for-the-entertainment-dollar": {"n":24,"task":"The Fan Persona Profile","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-93","text":"I can explain what motivates a fan by describing my persona's typical week and how much of their budget one outing costs, and why their top motivator fits them."},{"id":"8177-94","text":"I can analyze competition for attendance by naming one local indirect competitor that threatens my persona and a promotion that fits their motivator and budget to win them over."},{"id":"8177-2","text":"I can solve a problem by explaining how my promotion gets my persona off the couch and away from the competitor while staying within their budget."}],"parts":["You describe your persona: who they are, what a typical week looks like for them, and how much of their monthly budget one outing to your event would cost.","You pick one of the five motivators as your persona's top reason to go out and explain, with details from their life, why it fits them.","You name one specific local indirect competitor that could win your persona's money on a Friday night instead of your event.","You describe one specific promotion or experience the event could offer and say why it would make your persona choose the event over your competitor."]},
  "8177-the-bottom-line": {"n":25,"task":"The $10,000 Tip-Off Budget","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-98","text":"I can build an event budget by choosing my fee, ticket and concession prices, calculating revenue and every expense in Google Sheets, and reaching at least a 15% profit margin."},{"id":"8177-85","text":"I can identify the parts of a budget by listing every expense from the price list as its own line and labeling each a fixed or variable cost."},{"id":"8177-88","text":"I can analyze a budget by calculating net profit and profit margin, showing both versions if I missed 15%, and naming two big risks and my plan for each."},{"id":"8177-16","text":"I can protect financial information by sharing my budget sheet only with my teacher and keeping its numbers confidential."},{"id":"8177-34","text":"I can follow safe sharing practices by setting my Google Sheet so only my teacher can open the link I add to this document."},{"id":"8177-87","text":"I can calculate event expenses correctly from the price list, like the referee hours, the gym fee and the cost of the player t-shirts."}],"parts":["You choose a team fee, ticket price and concession price, estimate how many of each you'll sell (fewer if you raise prices), and calculate revenue from each source.","You list every expense from the price list as its own line with the right amount (like the referee hours and 96 t-shirts at $8) and a correct total.","Your sheet has every revenue and expense line, uses formulas for the totals, net profit and margin, and your final version reaches a profit margin of at least 15%.","Your memo to the board names the two biggest financial risks in your budget, using its numbers (like the ticket count where you'd lose money), and what you'd do about each."]},
  "8177-the-final-whistle": {"n":26,"task":"The Free Agent Portfolio","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-108","text":"I can plan my career by building one plan for my first job from three career paths, five resume bullets, three STAR stories, my network, and strategies for two barriers."},{"id":"8177-43","text":"I can evaluate career options by describing what each of my three jobs involves and naming the class project that prepared me for it."},{"id":"8177-104","text":"I can identify the education and training each of my three jobs usually requires, such as a degree, an internship, a certification or experience."},{"id":"8177-12","text":"I can tell my own story to an employer by writing three STAR stories from this year that answer interview questions about a time I solved a problem."},{"id":"8177-13","text":"I can show how I've grown by pointing to something in my resume bullets or STAR stories that I learned or got better at as the year went on."},{"id":"8177-32","text":"I can explain the value of membership by naming one professional organization, like DECA, I could join and how being a member could help my career."},{"id":"8177-101","text":"I can evaluate an event's success by writing a SWOT analysis of a real school event with at least two of each and naming one thing I'd measure next time."},{"id":"8177-102","text":"I can find career information by naming a real source for each of my three jobs, like a job posting, a careers page or the Occupational Outlook Handbook."},{"id":"8177-103","text":"I can map career levels by choosing three different jobs and explaining where each fits, from a first job I could get soon to one I'd work up to."},{"id":"8177-105","text":"I can plan around barriers by naming two things that could make my first job hard to get and a specific strategy to get past each one."},{"id":"8177-106","text":"I can find ways to grow professionally by naming one person or organization I could ask for an informational interview and how it could help me in the field."},{"id":"8177-107","text":"I can research a prospective employer through its job posting or careers page and say what the job there involves."}],"parts":["You write a SWOT analysis of a real school event with at least two strengths, two weaknesses, two opportunities and two threats, and name one thing you'd measure next time.","You name three different jobs in sports, entertainment or events, and for each say in a sentence what it involves and which project from this class prepared you for it.","You write five true resume bullets from projects in this class, each starting with an action verb, describing a specific task, and giving a result or number where you can.","You write three STAR stories from this year, each with its situation, task, action and result, that could answer a question like \"Tell me about a time you solved a problem.\"","You name one professional organization, like DECA, you could join and explain how being a member could help your career.","You name two things that could make it hard to get your first job in sports, entertainment or events, and a strategy for getting past each one."]},
  "8177-the-franchise-evolution": {"n":27,"task":"The Brand Revival Pitch","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-86","text":"I can identify new revenue sources by creating a priced bundle, a brand extension and a naming-rights deal that earn the Kickers money without selling more regular tickets."},{"id":"8177-46","text":"I can plan on-site revenue by pricing my bundle item by item with the bundle price and fan's savings, and explaining why my local business fits its stadium area."},{"id":"8177-23","text":"I can plan for growth by explaining which product life cycle stage the Kickers are in and how my three ideas help them grow again."},{"id":"8177-54","text":"I can spot an entrepreneurship opportunity by explaining how my brand extension is a new business the Kickers could launch and who would buy it."},{"id":"8177-76","text":"I can explain how licensing works by showing how my brand extension uses the Kickers name and logo and how the team could earn a royalty from it."}],"parts":["You list everything in your bundle with each item's price on its own, the bundle price, and how much the fan saves, and the math is right.","You propose one new product or service that has nothing to do with playing soccer and explain how it uses the Kickers brand and who would buy it.","You pick one area of the stadium and a real local business (not alcohol, tobacco, vaping or gambling) that should pay to put its name on it.","In two or three sentences, you name the Kickers' stage of the product life cycle, with a reason from their story, and say how your three ideas help them grow again."]},
  "8177-the-front-lines": {"n":28,"task":"The Front Lines","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-9","text":"I can recover a customer's experience by writing what I'd say at each T.H.A.N.K.S. step to the fan behind the pillar, including the solution I offer."},{"id":"8177-60","text":"I can respond to a customer's needs by scripting all six selling steps for the dealership owner, with two open-ended questions and three suite features turned into benefits."},{"id":"8177-61","text":"I can explain a policy decision to a customer by showing why the solution I offer the fan behind the pillar is fair to both the fan and the team."},{"id":"8177-41","text":"I can explain how winning the suite sale and fixing the pillar complaint each help the team succeed with its customers."},{"id":"8177-59","text":"I can explain customer retention by showing how my solution for the upset fan could keep them coming back to games instead of leaving for good."}],"parts":["You write what you'll say at each of the six steps of the selling process, with at least two open-ended questions and three suite features turned into benefits for this owner.","You write what you'll say to the fan behind the pillar at each step of the T.H.A.N.K.S. method, including the solution you offer.","For each scenario, you write the lines you used, what your partner said back and how the scene ended, then name the hardest step, a surprise, and how you adjusted."]},
  "8177-the-nil-deal": {"n":29,"task":"The NIL Activation","m":2,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-42","text":"I can justify a sponsorship choice by explaining why my public figure's audience beats someone with more followers, giving one advantage and one risk, and naming the numbers I'll track."},{"id":"8177-75","text":"I can follow advertising law by including a clear, easy-to-see #ad or #sponsored FTC disclosure on my post mockup."},{"id":"8177-20","text":"I can act professionally by choosing a public figure whose image fits CHHS's core values and labeling any mockup with a real person or logo “For educational purposes only.”"}],"parts":["You name a real public figure, describe who their followers are, explain why that audience matches your product's buyers, and say why they beat someone with more followers.","You say whether your choice is a macro-influencer or a micro-influencer and explain one advantage and one risk of that choice for your product.","Your post mockup has a clear #ad or #sponsored disclosure that's easy to see.","You name the numbers you'll track, like promo code uses, sales or attendance, and say what result would show the deal worked."]},
  "8177-the-sponsorship-negotiation": {"n":30,"task":"The Sponsorship Negotiation","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-6","text":"I can resolve conflict by answering the two most likely objections with Feel, Felt, Found and explaining how I stayed calm and pivoted when my partner said no."},{"id":"8177-8","text":"I can build diverse partnerships by including two businesses in my CRM owned by women or minority-group members or serving Richmond's diverse communities, and saying why each fits."},{"id":"8177-17","text":"I can use a CRM tool by building my tracker in Google Sheets with all five columns, five real local businesses and a status for each, and adding the link."},{"id":"8177-3","text":"I can take initiative by researching five real local businesses on my own and filling in each one's contact person, title and contact information."},{"id":"8177-41","text":"I can plan a pitch around a partner's needs by explaining in my CRM why each business is a good fit."},{"id":"8177-86","text":"I can explain the value of a sponsorship by saying in my pitch what the business gets for its $500 banner sponsorship."}],"parts":["Your sheet has all five columns (business, contact and title, contact information, why they fit, status) and five real local businesses, each with a status from the list.","You write how you'll answer the two objections you're most likely to hear, each with all three steps of Feel, Felt, Found.","You write each objection your partner raised and how you answered, then explain how you stayed calm and pivoted after the no, and whether you closed the deal."]},
  "8177-the-ticket-office": {"n":31,"task":"The Sales Management Playbook","m":3,"iCan":[{"id":"8177-41","text":"I can manage a sales team by setting each rep's quota, designing a contest with at least one non-money prize, and planning two support activities to help Alex succeed."},{"id":"8177-19","text":"I can calculate a sales goal by showing how many $750 plans it takes to reach $60,000 and how the reps' quotas add up to that total."},{"id":"8177-63","text":"I can identify ethical issues in sales by writing a three-rule code of ethics for my team and explaining the problem each rule prevents."},{"id":"8177-39","text":"I can explain my role as director of ticket sales in setting goals for, motivating and supporting my four reps."},{"id":"8177-40","text":"I can use a manager's judgment by explaining why each quota is challenging but possible for that rep, like giving Taylor, who just started, a different goal than Jordan."},{"id":"8177-42","text":"I can justify management decisions by explaining why I chose each rep's quota and my contest prize, and why those choices will work for the team."},{"id":"8177-62","text":"I can explain how my two support activities would give Alex useful, constructive feedback to improve, not just tell Alex they're behind."}],"parts":["You show how many $750 plans it takes to reach $60,000 (80) and give each rep a quota, with the math showing the quotas add up to at least that.","You design a contest for a slow month: how reps win, the prizes (at least one that isn't money), and why it will motivate this team.","You write a three-rule code of ethics for your sales team and explain the problem each rule prevents, like high-pressure sales or false promises.","You describe two specific support activities for Alex, like better leads, sales training or practice scripts, and how each would help Alex reach quota."]}
};
